# i18n: No hardcoded user-facing strings

All user-facing text in this project must come from `app/src/main/res/values*/strings.xml` and be referenced through the i18n system. Any new or modified feature must follow this — never introduce English (or any language) string literals into UI code.

## Sources of truth

- Default English: `app/src/main/res/values/strings.xml` (always update this).
- Per-locale overrides: `app/src/main/res/values-<locale>/strings.xml` (translations are managed via Crowdin; missing locales fall back to the default values, which is acceptable for new keys).

## Composables and Activities

```kotlin
// BAD — hardcoded literal in UI
Text("Center on Map")
Icon(..., contentDescription = "Close")

// GOOD — pulled from resources
Text(stringResource(R.string.action_center_on_map))
Icon(..., contentDescription = stringResource(R.string.cd_close))
```

## ViewModels and non-Composable code

Use the injected `appContext` (already available in `MapViewModel`) — never pass strings down from Composables or hardcode them:

```kotlin
// BAD
poiName = "Dropped Pin"

// GOOD
poiName = appContext.getString(R.string.dropped_pin_title)
```

## When adding a new string

1. Add the key to `values/strings.xml` and `values-en-rUS/strings.xml`.
2. Reference it via `R.string.<key>` (`stringResource(...)` in Compose, `getString(...)` in Kotlin).
3. Do not hand-translate other locales; Crowdin syncs them. English fallback in the meantime is fine.

## What is exempt

- Log tags / `android.util.Log` messages (developer-only).
- Internal identifiers: layer ids (`"poi-marker"`), asset names (`"marker"`, `"marker-stroked"`), JSON keys, preference keys.
- Test fixtures and `BuildConfig` values.
- `contentDescription = null` is allowed when an adjacent `Text` already provides the accessibility name for an icon.

If you are unsure whether a string is user-facing, treat it as user-facing and add a resource.
